WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:07.440
Okay, now moving on to the last one in this group of commands.

00:07.440 --> 00:11.200
So now so far what you have done, we have created something new in the database.

00:11.200 --> 00:14.680
We have changed the definition of something inside our database.

00:14.720 --> 00:18.680
And now the last one you can go and drop something from the database.

00:18.720 --> 00:21.880
Let's say we have another table and we don't need it anymore.

00:21.880 --> 00:28.000
So we can go and use the drop command in order to remove the table completely from the database.

00:28.000 --> 00:32.360
And this means as well removing everything that table and the data inside it.

00:32.360 --> 00:36.080
So now let's go to SQL and let's drop something from our database okay.

00:36.120 --> 00:40.000
So now our task says delete the table persons from the database.

00:40.000 --> 00:44.720
This is the simplest form of command in SQL but yet the most risky one.

00:44.720 --> 00:48.520
So what we need we have to delete and drop the whole table persons.

00:48.520 --> 00:49.760
We don't need it anymore.

00:49.800 --> 00:56.320
We're going to say drop table and then all what we have to do is to give the name of the table persons.

00:56.320 --> 01:01.080
So three words we don't have to specify anything, just destroy the table persons.

01:01.120 --> 01:02.560
Let's go and execute it.

01:02.600 --> 01:03.440
It is successful.

01:03.440 --> 01:05.000
So as you can see it is very simple.

01:05.040 --> 01:10.200
Now on the left side to your database go refresh and go to the tables and you will not see the table

01:10.240 --> 01:10.880
persons.

01:10.880 --> 01:11.960
So the drop command.

01:11.960 --> 01:14.440
It is very simple but yet very risky.

01:14.440 --> 01:20.400
So if you compare now create table with a drop table, you can see destroying things is way easier than

01:20.440 --> 01:21.280
building it.

01:21.280 --> 01:27.560
Those are the commands create, alter, drop those commands we use in order to define the structure

01:27.560 --> 01:30.320
of our database, the DDL commands.

01:30.360 --> 01:31.320
That's why it's very simple.

01:31.360 --> 01:31.800
All right.

01:31.800 --> 01:34.480
So that's all about the data definition language DDL.

01:34.520 --> 01:37.800
And with that you have learned how to define new stuff in your database.

01:37.800 --> 01:41.440
Now moving on to the next one we're going to learn about the data manipulation language.

01:41.440 --> 01:44.920
And here we can learn how to manipulate our data inside the database.

01:44.920 --> 01:45.480
Let's go.
